### The Problem: Naive Streaming Kills Framerates

When connecting to a streaming LLM endpoint via `EventSource` or `fetch` + `ReadableStream`, chunks arrive rapidly—often 60 to 120 chunks per second. 

A common beginner pattern is:
```typescript
// ❌ Anti-pattern: setState on every incoming chunk
reader.read().then(function processText({ done, value }) {
  if (done) return;
  const chunk = decoder.decode(value);
  setText(prev => prev + chunk); // Re-renders the entire component tree!
  return reader.read().then(processText);
});
```

At 80 chunks per second, React attempts 80 reconciliations per second. The browser's main thread spends 100% of its time recalculating DOM layouts, starving the event loop. User interactions stutter, scrolling freezes, and input fields drop keystrokes.

### The Solution: rAF-Synchronized Token Buffering

Instead of updating state on every network packet, accumulate incoming tokens in a mutable `useRef` queue and flush to React state only when the browser is ready to paint a new frame (via `requestAnimationFrame`):

```typescript
// ✅ Production Pattern: rAF Buffered Streaming
const tokenBufferRef = useRef('');
const isFlushingRef = useRef(false);
const [streamedText, setStreamedText] = useState('');

const onChunkReceived = (chunk: string) => {
  tokenBufferRef.current += chunk;

  if (!isFlushingRef.current) {
    isFlushingRef.current = true;
    requestAnimationFrame(() => {
      setStreamedText(prev => prev + tokenBufferRef.current);
      tokenBufferRef.current = '';
      isFlushingRef.current = false;
    });
  }
};
```

### Why This Works

1. **Screen Refresh Alignment:** Browsers refresh at 60Hz (16.6ms) or 120Hz (8.3ms). Rendering faster than the screen can refresh produces 0 visual benefit and 100% computational waste.
2. **Batching:** 10 network packets arriving within a 16ms window are batched into a single React re-render.
3. **Guaranteed 60 FPS:** The main thread remains responsive to user clicks, scrolling, and keyboard input.
